<% content_for :title, "What Am I Escaping? | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-escaping-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-hero">
    <div class="addiction-escaping-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-escaping-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / WHAT AM I ESCAPING?
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>WHAT</span>
        <span>AM I</span>
        <span>ESCAPING?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-escaping-intro">
        MAYBE IT WASN'T JUST ABOUT THE DRINK.<br>
        MAYBE I WANTED OUT.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">01 / UNDERNEATH IT</p>

      <h2>
        THE DRINK<br>
        WAS DOING<br>
        A JOB.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Addiction isn't always just about wanting the substance.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes the substance or behavior is doing something for you.
      </p>

      <p>
        Alcohol can numb something.
        Weed can change your mental state.
        Shopping can create excitement.
        Spending can create a rush.
      </p>

      <p>
        The behavior may be destructive and still have a function.
      </p>

      <p>
        Understanding that function doesn't excuse what happened.
        It gives you something more useful to work with than
        <strong>"Why the fuck am I like this?"</strong>
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        DON'T JUST ASK:<br>
        "WHY DID I DO IT?"<br><br>
        ASK:<br>
        "WHAT DID IT DO FOR ME?"
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">02 / ESCAPE</p>

      <h2>
        I DON'T<br>
        WANT TO<br>
        FEEL THIS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Sometimes addiction is less about chasing pleasure
        and more about escaping discomfort.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-escape-grid">

        <div>
          <span>PAIN</span>
          <p>
            I want a break from what my body is making me feel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>DEPRESSION</span>
          <p>
            I don't want to feel empty, heavy, hopeless, or exhausted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>ANXIETY</span>
          <p>
            I want my brain to shut the fuck up for a minute.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>SHAME</span>
          <p>
            I don't want to sit here thinking about what I've done.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>LONELINESS</span>
          <p>
            I don't want to feel alone with myself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>BOREDOM</span>
          <p>
            I want something — anything — to happen.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        SOMETIMES "I WANT A DRINK"<br>
        REALLY MEANS<br>
        "I WANT TO FEEL DIFFERENT."
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-black">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">03 / PHYSICAL PAIN</p>

      <h2>
        GET ME<br>
        OUT OF MY<br>
        BODY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Physical pain can make escape incredibly appealing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pain doesn't stay neatly inside the body.
      </p>

      <p>
        It can fuck with sleep.
        Limit what you can do.
        Make you angry.
        Make you restless.
        Make you bored.
        Wear down your patience.
      </p>

      <p>
        Eventually, the thought may not even be:
        <strong>"I want alcohol."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        It may be:
        <strong>"I need a fucking break from this."</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        THE REAL NEED:<br>
        RELIEF.
      </div>

      <p>
        That's an important distinction.
      </p>

      <p>
        Because if the need is relief, recovery means finding
        safer ways to respond to that need instead of pretending
        the need doesn't exist.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-purple">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">04 / DEPRESSION</p>

      <h2>
        MAKE ME<br>
        FEEL<br>
        SOMETHING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Depression can make escape look like relief.
      </p>

      <p>
        When everything feels flat, heavy, pointless, or exhausting,
        changing your state can become the entire objective.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe you want numbness.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe you want excitement.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe you just want the next few hours to feel different
        from the last few.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-thought">
        I DON'T NEED TO FEEL GOOD.<br>
        I JUST DON'T WANT TO FEEL LIKE THIS.
      </div>

      <p>
        That's why understanding the emotional function matters.
      </p>

      <p>
        If depression is underneath the craving,
        fighting only the craving leaves the depression sitting there
        waiting for another opportunity.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">05 / MANIA</p>

      <h2>
        MAYBE I'M<br>
        NOT TRYING<br>
        TO ESCAPE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Not every addictive behavior is about escaping something painful.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes it's about chasing something.
      </p>

      <p>
        More excitement.
        More intensity.
        More stimulation.
        More pleasure.
        More risk.
        More fucking everything.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania or hypomania can make restraint feel unnecessary
        and consequences feel very far away.
      </p>

      <p>
        Alcohol, weed, shopping, spending, and other impulsive behavior
        can become part of keeping the intensity going.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        SOMETIMES THE QUESTION ISN'T:<br>
        "WHAT AM I ESCAPING?"<br><br>
        IT'S:<br>
        "WHAT AM I CHASING?"
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">06 / THE RUSH</p>

      <h2>
        MAYBE I<br>
        WANTED TO<br>
        FEEL ALIVE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        The substance isn't the only thing that can create a rush.
      </p>

      <p>
        Buying something can.
      </p>

      <p>
        Spending money can.
      </p>

      <p>
        Taking a risk can.
      </p>

      <p>
        Getting away with something can.
      </p>

      <p>
        The anticipation itself can become part of the reward.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-cycle">
        <span>WANT</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>CHASE</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>RUSH</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>CRASH</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>WANT AGAIN</span>
      </div>

      <p>
        If you only look at the final behavior,
        you can miss the fact that the chase itself was doing something.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        SOMETIMES I WASN'T ESCAPING LIFE.<br>
        I WAS ESCAPING NOT FEELING ALIVE ENOUGH.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-purple">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">07 / THE AFTERMATH</p>

      <h2>
        THEN I<br>
        HAD MORE<br>
        TO ESCAPE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Here's the fucked-up part: escape can create more things
        you want to escape from.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-loop">

        <div>
          <span>PAIN</span>
          <p>I want out.</p>
        </div>

        <b>→</b>

        <div>
          <span>ESCAPE</span>
          <p>I drink, use, spend, or act impulsively.</p>
        </div>

        <b>→</b>

        <div>
          <span>CONSEQUENCE</span>
          <p>Money, conflict, shame, legal problems, relapse.</p>
        </div>

        <b>→</b>

        <div>
          <span>MORE PAIN</span>
          <p>Now I want out again.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        The behavior solves something temporarily
        while creating another problem behind it.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        THE ESCAPE BECAME<br>
        ANOTHER FUCKING CAGE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-black">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">08 / GET SPECIFIC</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DID<br>
        I ACTUALLY<br>
        WANT?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        "I wanted to drink" is an answer.
        It's just not always the deepest one.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-questions">

        <div>
          <span>WHAT WAS I FEELING?</span>
          <p>
            Physically and emotionally.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT DID I WANT TO STOP?</span>
          <p>
            Pain? Thoughts? Shame? Boredom? Sadness? Anxiety?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT DID I WANT TO START?</span>
          <p>
            Excitement? Confidence? Numbness? Energy? Connection?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT WAS MY MOOD DOING?</span>
          <p>
            Was I depressed, manic, restless, irritable, or exhausted?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT DID I EXPECT IT TO GIVE ME?</span>
          <p>
            What was the promise?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>DID IT ACTUALLY WORK?</span>
          <p>
            And if it did, for how fucking long?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">09 / THE NEED</p>

      <h2>
        KEEP<br>
        THE NEED.<br>
        CHANGE THE MOVE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Recovery doesn't require pretending you no longer need relief,
        comfort, excitement, connection, or escape.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those needs are still there.
      </p>

      <p>
        The question becomes whether the old behavior
        is the only way you know how to answer them.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-needs">

        <div>
          <span>I NEED RELIEF.</span>
          <p>
            What else can reduce what I'm carrying?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>I NEED ESCAPE.</span>
          <p>
            How can I change my environment without blowing up my life?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>I NEED CONNECTION.</span>
          <p>
            Who can I get around instead of disappearing?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>I NEED EXCITEMENT.</span>
          <p>
            Where can I get stimulation without creating another disaster?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>I NEED MY BRAIN TO SLOW DOWN.</span>
          <p>
            What actually helps me respond to the state I'm in?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>I NEED HELP.</span>
          <p>
            Who needs to know what's happening before it gets worse?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        THE NEED ISN'T THE ENEMY.<br>
        THE OLD SOLUTION MIGHT BE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 10 -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">10 / STOP RUNNING</p>

      <h2>
        TURN<br>
        AROUND<br>
        AND LOOK.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Escape works by keeping you moving away from something.
      </p>

      <p>
        Recovery sometimes means turning around long enough
        to see what has been chasing you.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pain.
        Depression.
        Mania.
        Shame.
        Loneliness.
        Anger.
        Fear.
        Boredom.
        Your own thoughts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Seeing it doesn't mean you suddenly know how to fix it.
      </p>

      <p>
        But now you're dealing with the actual problem instead
        of only fighting the behavior that grew around it.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        IF I KNOW WHAT I'M RUNNING FROM,<br>
        I CAN FINALLY START DEALING WITH IT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="ae-ending-small">
        MAYBE THE QUESTION WAS NEVER JUST:
        "WHY DO I WANT IT?"
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DO<br>
        I NEED<br>
        <span>RIGHT NOW?</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="ae-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Maybe I need relief.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe I need connection.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe I need excitement.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe I'm depressed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe my mood is climbing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe I'm in pain and fucking exhausted from dealing with it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Whatever the answer is, knowing what I'm actually trying
        to change gives me more options than automatically reaching
        for the same old escape hatch.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "ae-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>